===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*tak-}}
{{uder|en|la|reticēns}}, present participle of {{m|la|reticeō||to keep silence}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛtɪsənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-reticent.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-reticent.ogg|a=AU}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Keeping one's [[thought]]s and [[opinion]]s to [[oneself]]; [[reserved]] or [[restrained]].
#* {{RQ:Emerson English Traits|[[s:English Traits/Result|Result]]|text=They are slow and '''reticent''', and are like a dull good horse which lets every nag pass him, but with whip and spur will run down every racer in the field.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Edwin Drood|XXIII|text=But he was a '''reticent''' as well as an eccentric man; and he made no mention of a certain evening when he warmed his hands at the gatehouse fire, and looked steadily down upon a certain heap of torn and miry clothes upon the floor.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|LIV|text=She had told him she was not now at Marlott, but had been curiously '''reticent''' as to her actual address, and the only course was to go to Marlott and inquire for it.}}
#* {{RQ:Buchan Thirty-Nine Steps|3|text=The milkman had been released, I read, and the true criminal, about whose identity the police were '''reticent''', was believed to have got away from London by one of the northern lines.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:H. P. Lovecraft|title=s:The Music of Erich Zann|text=The inhabitants of that street impressed me peculiarly; At first I thought it was because they were all silent and '''reticent'''; but later decided it was because they were all very old.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:Rafael Sabatini|title=[[s:Captain Blood|Captain Blood: His Odyssy]]|chapter=chapter XXV<!--This is how the title is spelled in Wikisource -->|text=But they were not '''reticent''' enough to prevent the circulation of certain uneasy rumours and extravagant stories of discreditable adventures.}}
# {{lb|en|proscribed}} [[hesitant|Hesitant]] or not [[wanting]] to take some [[action]]; [[reluctant]] (usually followed by a verb in the infinitive).
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2010|author=Helene J. Sinnreich|title=Sexual Violence against Jewish Women during the Holocaust|chapter=The Rape of Jewish Women during the Holocaust|page=108|text=While many uncomfortable components of the Holocaust have been analyzed in minute detail, the rape of Jewish women during this era persists as a subject that scholars and victims alike are '''reticent''' to explore.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=C. Dallett Hemphill|title=Siblings: Brothers and Sisters in American History|chapter=chapter 3|text=One letter from Deborah presents an especially fascinating contrast with Jane's letters to her brother. Whereas Jane was keen on discussing politics, Deborah was '''reticent''' to do so.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Michael Naas|title=A Companion to Derrida|editor=Zeynep Direk; Leonard Lawlor|page=236|text=But I would now like to argue that there was for Derrida a privileged site in Ancient Philosophy for this question, one to which Derrida would repeatedly return in his writing and thinking – Socrates’ denigration or denunciation of writing, his attempt in the Phaedrus to exclude writing from thinking and philosophy proper. As I suggested at the outset, this claim regarding Derrida’s relation to the Greeks is one that Derrida himself would have been '''reticent''' to accept.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Ray Bull|title=Investigative Interviewing|page=3|text=The police may be '''reticent''' to charge the alleged offender, prosecutors '''reticent''' to continue with the prosecution, and juries '''reticent''' to convict.}}

====Usage notes====
* The second sense of ''reticent'' has developed in the years since the end of the Second World War and is still not universally accepted as correct usage. However, of the major English-language dictionaries, [[w:Merriam-Webster|Merriam-Webster]] does recognize the newer sense [http://www.merriam-webster.com/words-at-play/can-reticent-mean-reluctant].

====Synonyms====
* (''sense 1'') {{l|en|reserved}}, {{l|en|restrained}}, {{l|en|tight-lipped}} (See also [[Thesaurus:taciturn]])
* (''sense 2'') {{l|en|reluctant}}, {{l|en|unwilling}}, {{l|en|disinclined}}, {{l|en|loath}}

